  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Friday)\nIssued at 101 PM MST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nCurrent radar imagery shows light showers/sprinkles over \nsouthwestern into south central SD behind a weak cold front that's\npushing through central SD. Breezy northwest winds continue \nacross the western SD plains with sfc stations reporting gusts of \n25 to 35 mph. These breezy winds will continue through the rest of\nthe afternoon before diminishing after sunset.\n\nUnsettled northwest flow will continue over the area through \ntomorrow with another, slightly stronger shortwave and attendant \nfront moving through the region. This system will bring more \nchances for light rain/snow to northeastern WY into the Black \nHills and southwestern SD later tonight through early afternoon \ntomorrow. Forecast soundings over the Black Hills into \nnortheastern WY support snow through this event with northeastern \nWY picking up light accumulations of up to an inch. The higher \nelevations of the Black Hills may see a little more accumulation \n(up to 3 inches) but we're not expecting any significant \naccumulations.\n\nMeanwhile, southwestern into south central SD may see snow early \nin the morning but as the day warms up, that should transition \nover to rain with total rainfall generally being around 0.01-0.1\".\nAs the system pushes through, northwest winds will pick up across\nthe western SD plains. Modest 3hr pressure rises of 3 to 5 mb and\nCAA will support gusts of 35-45 mph over much of the western SD \nplains with gusts of up to 50 to 55 mph over portions of \nnorthwestern SD. While some spots may see a brief period of \nadvisory level gusts, not confident enough in duration and \ncoverage of these gusts to hoist any wind headlines.\n\nHeight rises behind the exiting shortwave will promote a dry day \nacross the region for Sunday with highs ranging from the low 30s \nto 40s. Looking beyond this weekend, upper level flow will \ntransition to more zonal flow early next week. This translates to \nhighs in the 40s to 50s and mostly dry weather.",
